\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc2}{\*\bkmkend toc2}\b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Higher On Fresh Dlr Supply From Exporters \b0\par\par\uc2 206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2001\par 05:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China's yuan ended stronger against the U.S. dollar Monday after two sessions of declines as exporters sold their excess dollars to the banks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at 8.2768 yuan, down from CNY8.2770 Friday. The dollar moved between CNY8.2765 and CNY8.2769 during the session on modest volume of $190 million. \par \par \uc2  "Sell (dollar) orders dominated most of the session," a trader at Shanghai Pudong Development Bank said. \par \par \uc2  Most exporters are required to sell 85% of their export receipts to state-owned banks. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only be converted for current account transactions. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has many times settled slightly outside the implicit trading band of CNY8.2770-CNY8.2800 since the start of the year, almost without any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The trend indicates that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is testing a wider trading band on the eve of its entry into the World Trade Organization. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen rose to CNY6.7926 per 100 yen from CNY6.7823 Friday, while the Hong Kong dollar edged lower to CNY1.0604 from CNY1.0606. \par \par \uc2  -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218-3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011105dxb50006v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
