\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc5}{\*\bkmkend toc5}\b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Flat; Some Foreign Banks Sidelined \b0\par\par\uc2 239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2001\par 05:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China's yuan finished unchanged against the U.S. dollar Wednesday in thin volume. \par \par \uc2  As elsewhere in Asia, some foreign banks in Shanghai didn't take \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  orders Wednesday because they aren't sure how they will settle their dollar payments after devastating terrorist attacks leveled the World Trade Center in New York. \par \par \uc2  Among the banks sidelined were ABN Amro and Bank of America. "We are afraid of delays in settling our receipts with U.S.-based banks" because of the attacks, said a trader at ABN Amro in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  "Trading was extremely slow this morning, since many banks decided to avoid trading," said a trader at Shanghai Pudong Development Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8.2769 yuan Wednesday, the same as Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  It traded between CNY8.2735 and CNY8.2772, but traders said CNY8.2735 was merely a stray deal and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan can only be converted for current account purposes, and most exporters must repatriate 85% of their export receipts. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  restricts wide movements in yuan trading. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar climbed to CNY1.0608 from Tuesday's CNY1.0606. The Japanese yen rose to CNY6.9010 per 100 yen from CNY6.7250. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218-3268;\par djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com\par -0- 12/09/01 03-47G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par 