\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc10}{\*\bkmkend toc10}\b \uc2  China's Yuan Ends Flat Amid Range-Bound Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 246 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2001\par 06:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China's yuan ended unchanged at CNY8.2768 to the dollar Monday, despite strong dollar sell orders from exporters, traders said. \par \par \uc2  A trader at Industrial & Commercial Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan from strengthening further. \par \par \uc2  Most exporters are required to sell 85% of their export receipts to state-owned banks, so there is constant pressure on the yuan to appreciate against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  China is still registering a trade surplus, although the gap has narrowed due to an economic slowdown hitting its trading partners. \par \par \uc2  The yuan traded between CNY8.2768 and CNY8.2773 on volume of $270 million, compared with $200 million Friday. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only for trade purposes, and China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strictly monitors its daily trading. As a result, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't move much, and speculation is virtually impossible. \par \par \uc2  China is generally thought to maintain an unofficial yuan trading band between CNY8.2770 and CNY8.2800 to the dollar. The range has been frequently breached in recent sessions, however, leading to speculation that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying out a wider band ahead of the country's WTO entry. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen slipped to CNY6.8672 per 100 yen from CNY6.8845. The Hong Kong dollar stood unchanged at CNY1.0606. \par \par \uc2  -By 