\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc12}{\*\bkmkend toc12}\b \uc2  China's yuan edges up, ignores export figures. \b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2001\par 07:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 13 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against the U.S. dollar on Friday after moving outside the strong end of its usual hair-thin trading band for the entire session due to steady dollar supplies, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The tightly-controlled market ignored official June trade figures showing exports fell for the first time in two years. \par \par \uc2  A strong supply of dollars in the market based on China's foreign trade surplus kept the yuan at high levels, but in the longer term, slowing foreign trade could exert downward pressure on the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at an intraday high of 8.2766 to the dollar, up from Thursday's 8.2768. It touched an intraday low of 8.2768, staying outside its narrow longstanding band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 throughout the session. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC), dealers said. \par \par \uc2  State media said after the market closed on Thursday that China's June exports were $22.08 billion, down 0.6 percent year on year, while imports rose 4.7 percent to $21.25 billion. \par \par \uc2  The trade surplus fell sharply to $830 million in June from $1.9 billion in June 2000. In the first half of 2001, China posted a surplus of $8.14 billion, down nearly 34 percent from a year earlier, state media said. \par \par \uc2  "The figures are disappointing," said a local bank dealer. "But China has continuously recorded trade surplus since the start of last year, so banks and exporters have a lot of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in hand. The impact of slowing trade has yet to be felt." \par \par \uc2  The trade surplus is key to dollar supply on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade companies have to sell most of their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues to domestic banks - who balance their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the national market - and buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from banks on the basis of import documents. \par \par \uc2  LONGER-TERM IMPACT SEEN \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the latest trade figures showed China was not completely immune to the global economic slowdown which has hit the economies of other Asian countries. \par \par \uc2  "We have seen trade figures that show China's exports to key trading partners, including the United States and Japan, have begun to drop since the second quarter," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "In the longer term, slowing exports and a decreasing trade surplus will exert downward pressure on the yuan," she said. \par \par \uc2  But in the near term, the yuan was likely to remain strong around the 8.2770 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Turnover on Friday was heavy at $530 million, up from $244 million on Thursday in line with the trend in the first half of 2001. Turnover in the first half was $30.167 billion, up 62 percent year on year, the official Shanghai Daily said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  From mid-1997 to April 2000, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had kept the yuan in a wafer-thin range of 8.2770 to 8.2800, partly to fend off speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But over the past 14 months, it has relaxed its grip to some extent and allowed the yuan to end outside that range 72 times, although only twice outside the weak end of the band. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan weakened to 6.6620 against 100 Japanese yen, compared with Thursday's 6.6535. It closed a notch firmer against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0606 from 1.0607. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx7d02gkx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
