\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc15}{\*\bkmkend toc15}\b \uc2  China's yuan ends down on importer dollar demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2001\par 07:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 11 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday due to late dollar buying by some domestic banks for importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign that the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support or depress the yuan on Wednesday. The bank acted to curb its strength on Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2775 to the dollar on Wednesday, down from Tuesday's 8.2770 after moving in a narrow range between 8.2768 and 8.2782. Turnover rose to $340 million from an already heavy $250 million on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan had traded outside the strong end of its narrow, longstanding trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 in early trade on Wednesday, but dollar demand was also heavy. \par \par \uc2  "There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action as strong dollar buying prevented the yuan from rising," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Late in the session, dollar buying from local banks on behalf of importers intensified, making the yuan close lower." \par \par \uc2  Chinese companies have to sell most of their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues to domestic banks and buy it from banks using import documents. Banks balance their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session to prevent the yuan from rising beyond 8.2768, dealers said. It was the first time the had bank acted to curb yuan strength since late last year, they said. \par \par \uc2  The PBOC holds tight control over movements of the yuan, which is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  From mid-1997 to April 2000, it sandwiched the yuan between 8.2770 and 8.2800 to fend off speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But over the past 14 months, it has relaxed its grip to some extent and allowed the yuan to end outside that range 70 times, mostly outside the firm end. \par \par \uc2  Officials say the yuan will be allowed to trade in a wider band to cope with changes to China's balance of payments after its entry to the World Trade Organisation. But they have not said when that might happen or what the new range would be. \par \par \uc2  China is expected to join the WTO by early 2002 at the latest and a rise in imports after entry coupled with slowing global demand for exports is likely to erode its trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  In a recent Reuters poll of 10 analysts, seven saw no change in 2001 to the current range, equivalent to a miniscule 0.02 percent up or down. One analyst saw a marginal widening to 0.04 percent while two expected a move of up to three percent. \par \par \uc2  The poll produced an average forecast of a yuan band of 2.78 percent up or down by the end of 2002. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to remain strong in the near term around the 8.2770 level due to ample dollar supply on the market. But they said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not likely to let it close stronger than 8.2760. \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks have accumulated heavy dollar positions as China has posted a trade surplus since the start of last year, although the surplus began to decrease this year as the global economy slowed, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  China reported a trade surplus of $7.32 billion in the first five months of 2001, down from $10.46 billion in the same period of last year. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan rose slightly to end at 6.5870 to 100 Japanese yen, compared with Tuesday's 6.5900. It closed unchanged at 1.0605 against the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010711dx7b00ahy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
