\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc16}{\*\bkmkend toc16}\b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  seen acting to cap yuan. \b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2001\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 10 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed a touch weaker against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday after the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yuan's strength, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the yuan on April 28, but has not capped yuan strength since late last year, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2770 to the dollar, down from Monday's 8.2767 after touching an intraday high of 8.2768 and a low of 8.2786. Turnover remained decent at $250 million, although that was down from $440 million on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan had traded outside the strong end of its narrow, longstanding trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 all of Monday and most of Tuesday due to a steady supply of dollars from domestic banks selling the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of exporters. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last 15 minutes of trade on Tuesday to prevent the yuan from rising beyond 8.2768, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "After trading and closing outside the band yesterday, the yuan continued its rising momentum today. I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session because this trend showed the yuan would have risen further," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is consistent with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of keeping the yuan stable," he said. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of China (PBOC) declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  YUAN STABILITY \par \par \uc2  The PBOC holds tight control over movements of the yuan, which is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  From mid-1997 to April 2000, it sandwiched the yuan between 8.2770 and 8.2800 to fend off speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But over the past 14 months, it has relaxed its grip to some extent and allowed the yuan to end outside that range 70 times, mostly outside the firm end. \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials have said the yuan will be allowed to trade in a wider band to cope with changes to China's balance of payments after its entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  But they have not said when that might happen or what the new range would be. \par \par \uc2  China is expected to join WTO by early 2002 at the latest and a rise in imports after entry coupled with slowing global demand for exports is likely to erode its trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the government did not want to see too strong a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it would make exports uncompetitive. \par \par \uc2  "A strong yuan might become difficult to bear as export growth has already slowed this year due to a global economic slowing," another bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  China reported a trade surplus of $7.32 billion in the first five months of 2001, down from $10.46 billion in the same period of last year. \par \par \uc2  In a recent Reuters poll of 10 analysts, seven saw no change in 2001 to the current range, equivalent to a miniscule 0.02 percent up or down. One analyst saw a marginal widening to 0.04 percent while two expected a move of up to three percent. \par \par \uc2  The poll produced an average forecast of a yuan band of 2.78 percent up or down by the end of 2002. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to remain strong in the near term around the 8.2770 level due to ample dollar supply on the market. They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not likely to let it close stronger than 8.2760. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan fell slightly to end at 6.5900 to 100 Japanese yen, compared with Monday's 6.5856. It also edged down against the Hong Kong dollar to close at 1.0605 from 1.0603. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010710dx7a00dwo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
