\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc19}{\*\bkmkend toc19}\b \uc2  ANALYSIS-WTO delays China's plans to widen yuan band. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tiffany Wu \par \uc2 1,011 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2001\par 07:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 2 (Reuters) - China is not expected to loosen its tight grip on the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  until next year, when its impending entry to the World Trade Organisation starts to undercut the country's trade surplus, analysts said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts have pushed back their forecasts of when China will give the yuan's slim trading band more girth, largely because it did not join the global trade body earlier this year as originally anticipated. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters poll of 10 analysts, seven saw no change in 2001 to the current range, equivalent to a sliver of 0.02 percent up or down. One analyst predicted a marginal widening to 0.04 percent and two expected a move of up to three percent. \par \par \uc2  Three percent would mean a band of 8.0301 to 8.5269 per dollar, a dramatic shift from the long-standing range of 8.2770 to 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  The average forecast was 0.52 percent, much narrower than the 2.7 percent predicted last December. The poll forecast a band of 2.78 percent up or down by end-2002. (Hit for poll). \par \par \uc2  Analysts say China must give the yuan more breathing space to counter trade shocks expected after it joins WTO or if the Japanese yen continues to weaken. \par \par \uc2  "I think China will be in WTO by November and will start widening the band some time in the first quarter of next year," said Frank Gong, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  He saw the yuan continuing to trade between 8.2760 and 8.2800 in 2001, broadening to 8.2500 to 8.6000 next year. \par \par \uc2  Senior government officials have linked a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with WTO entry, vowing to give the yuan more leeway to make exports more competitive after accession. \par \par \uc2  China's trade surplus will shrink further next year, as more foreign goods will be imported after it joins the WTO, global demand slows and the yen remains weak, economists said. \par \par \uc2  China had a trade surplus of $7.32 billion in the first five months of 2001, down 30 percent from the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  IN WTO SOON \par \par \uc2  China's 15-year WTO bid had been delayed by disputes on agriculture and services, but China cleared a major hurdle last month by clinching deals with the United States and the European Union. The only country yet to reach a bilateral agreement is Mexico, which has said it will not hold up China's membership. \par \par \uc2  "I think these agreements have raised the prospects of all outstanding issues of China's accession being resolved in the next few months," said Paul Schymyck, regional economist for IDEA Global in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Multilateral accession talks resume this week in Geneva. \par \par \uc2  Schymyck expects the yuan's band to be widened to three percent up or down as soon as this year. \par \par \uc2  "Initially it's likely to trade at the stronger end of that band because of the very strong capital inflows and portfolio inflows linked to FDI, as well as fairly strong positive sentiment towards the China story," he said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign direct investment is expected to accelerate in China as WTO rules remove barriers. China forecasts seven percent GDP growth this year, making it a safe haven during a global slowdown. \par \par \uc2  "On the balance-of-payments side, they should be able to maintain a surplus," said Pu Yonghao, Nomura International's senior economist for Greater China. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong-based Pu predicted a gradual relaxation of the yuan's band. "Liberalising the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an important step for liberalising the financial and capital markets, but they are not going to do it overnight," he said. \par \par \uc2  YEN THE WILDCARD \par \par \uc2  China-based analysts were generally more conservative, saying a stable exchange rate was central to China's economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Still, in the past 14 months China has eased its clasp on the yuan, letting it close 68 times outside the 8.2770 to 8.2800 box enforced since the Asian economic crisis hit in 1997. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers now regard 8.2760 as the strong end of the so-called band, although the weak floor was still 8.2800 since China has let the yuan close outside that mark only twice. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must loosen its grip and allow some depreciation to counter the deflationary pressures China will import next year, economists said. Cheaper foreign imports and tariff cuts will force local firms to cut prices to compete. \par \par \uc2  The wildcard is the yen , which has fallen about eight percent against the dollar so far this year to the 124.50 area, pushing other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down and making China's exports less competitive. Most economists viewed a further dramatic decline in the yen as unlikely, but noted the risk. \par \par \uc2  "If you have another 10 percent depreciation in the yen and on top of that you have to cut tariffs ... the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (yuan) has to be a little weaker to modify that," Bank of America's Gong said. \par \par \uc2  When the yen fell dramatically in 1998, China was praised for holding the yuan steady and gained political clout for preventing a round of competitive devaluations across the recession-hit region. \par \par \uc2  But Gong said the risk of a Chinese devaluation was greater during the current economic slowdown because the United States would be more willing to tolerate yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  In 1998, Japan and the United States jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the yen and effectively stopped the yuan from devaluing. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the Bush administration will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sake because that's very hard to sell - that you have to spend taxpayers' money to protect China. China is strategically less important to the Bush administration," Gong said. \par \par \uc2  While the Clinton administration gave China "face" for not devaluing, Bush was unlikely to do so, Gong said. "I don't think China has the same political reasons to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable if they are not getting the political returns back," he said. (With additional reporting by Brian Rhoads). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx7200bmm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
