\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc24}{\*\bkmkend toc24}\b \uc2  China's yuan ends up as exporters sell dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2001\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 6 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday to close outside the strong end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 as domestic banks sold dollars on behalf of exporters, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at its intraday high of 8.2769 to the dollar, compared with Tuesday's close of 8.2774. It moved narrowly during the session, touching a low of 8.2775. \par \par \uc2  Turnover remained heavy at $270 million, although that was down from $290 million on Tuesday, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 , the Web site of the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Domestic banks have seen more dollar settlement demand from exporters over the past few weeks, an indication of strong exports," said a bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Their dollar selling pushed up the yuan today," he said. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. Chinese trade firms are required to sell most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  income to domestic banks designated to trade on the market and buy hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from them using import documents. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said domestic banks had many dollars in hand after accumulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the start of last year due to China's healthy trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has been generally strong over the past week, trading tightly around the 8.2770 mark, the firm end of its normal thin 30-notch trade band due to flush dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the yuan to continue to move around 8.2770 in the near term and possibly close slightly outside the strong end of its normal thin trading band. \par \par \uc2  For about three years since mid-1997, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sandwiched the yuan within the band through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fend off speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Over the past year, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip and allowed the yuan to close outside the band 61 times, although only twice outside the weak end. There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Officials say the government is considering a wider range for the yuan's exchange rate to cushion trade shocks expected from the China's accession to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  But they have given no timetable or range. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan strengthened to close at 6.8651 to 100 yen versus 6.9322 on Tuesday. It ended almost unchanged against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0604 from 1.0605. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6602bbc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
