\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc25}{\*\bkmkend toc25}\b \uc2  China's yuan edges down but keeps strength. \b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2001\par 07:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 5 (Reuters) - China yuan weakened a touch against the U.S. dollar in heavy volume on Tuesday as some domestic banks bought dollars in the last few minutes of trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the yuan kept its strength for most of the session, supported by strong dollar supply on the market, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2774 to the dollar, compared with Monday's close of 8.2768. It moved mainly around 8.2770, the strong end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800, and touched an intraday high of 8.2763 and a low of 8.2783. \par \par \uc2  "The last few minutes of dollar buying by a handful of domestic banks made the yuan close down," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But the fall did not represent a trend as ample dollar supply kept the yuan around 8.2770 for most of the day," he said. \par \par \uc2  Turnover remained heavy at $290 million on Tuesday, although that was down from $350 million on Monday, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 , the Web site of the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said domestic banks had many dollars in hand as they had accumulated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the start of last year due to China's healthy trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to dollar supply on the market as China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Domestic banks usually settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the market after trading with their corporate clients, mainly exporters and importers. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has been generally strong over the past week, trading tightly around the 8.2770 mark, the firm end of its normal thin 30-notch trade band due to flush dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the yuan to continue to move around 8.2770 in the near term and possibly close slightly outside the strong end of its normal thin trading band. \par \par \uc2  For about three years since mid-1997, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sandwiched the yuan within the band through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fend off speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Over the past year, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip on the yuan and allowed it to close outside the band 60 times, although only twice outside the weak end. There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Officials say the government is considering a wider range for the yuan's exchange rate to cushion trade shocks expected from the China's accession to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  But they have given no timetable or range. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan strengthened to close at 6.9322 to 100 yen versus 6.9450 on Monday. It ended unchanged against the Hong Kong $1.0605. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6502eet\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
