\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc28}{\*\bkmkend toc28}\b \uc2  China yuan ends flat in heavy trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2001\par 07:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 15 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed unchanged at 8.2769 against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday, outside the firm end of its tiny trade band for the second time this week as dollar supply from local exporters remained solid. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said yuan trade was largely flat on Tuesday although turnover rose to a robust $330 million from a moderate $202 million. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan was relatively stable today and most deals were done at round 8.2770," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. Banks settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly exporters and importers. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has been firming since last week as banks keep unloading the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a sign that Beijing was recording a healthy balance of payments. \par \par \uc2  State media said on Tuesday China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were expected to grew steadily and the yuan would remain stable this year as the country's current account could stay in surplus. \par \par \uc2  "China's current account is expected to maintain a surplus in 2001 but its size could fall, while capital and financial accounts could be roughly balanced," the Financial News quoted a statement from the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) as saying. \par \par \uc2  China had a current account surplus of $20.52 billion in 2000, the Financial News reported on Tuesday, compared with a current surplus of $15.67 billion in 1999 and a surplus of $29.32 billion in 1998. \par \par \uc2  The capital account surplus stood at $1.92 billion in 2000, down by $5.72 billion from in 1999, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-1997, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sandwich the yuan in the trade band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to deter speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But over the past year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has allowed the yuan to close outside the wafer-thin band 54 times, although only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to rein in the firming yuan on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials say the government is considering making the rigid exchange regime more flexible to cushion trade shocks after it joins the World Trade Organisation, possibly this year. \par \par \uc2  That may include a wider trade band for the yuan, although officials have not given a timetable or new range. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan gained ground to close at 6.7078 to 100 yen versus 6.7502. It also strengthened against the Hong Kong dollar to end at 1.0605, compared with 1.0610 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx5f0058h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
