\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc29}{\*\bkmkend toc29}\b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China's 2000 current account surplus up. \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2001\par 03:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, May 15 (Reuters) - China had a current account surplus of $20.52 billion in 2000, the Financial News reported on Tuesday, citing data of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE). \par \par \uc2  That compared with a current surplus of $15.67 billion in 1999 and a surplus of $29.32 billion in 1998, according to earlier official figures. \par \par \uc2  It was the seventh consecutive year in which China registered a surplus on the account, which covers mainly trade and services. \par \par \uc2  China registered a surplus of $34.47 billion in trade of goods last year and posted a deficit of $5.6 billion in services, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  China posted a capital account surplus of $1.92 billion in 2000, down by $5.72 billion from in 1999, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  The capital account mainly covers investment and borrowings. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves swelled by $10.9 billion last year to $165.57 billion at the end of 2000, it said. \par \par \uc2  The reserves were $175.85 billion at the end of March this year, according to the latest official data. \par \par \uc2  "China's current account is expected to maintain a surplus in 2001 but its size could fall, while capital and financial accounts could be roughly balanced," the Financial News quoted a SAFE statement as saying. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are expected to grew steadily and the renminbi exchange rate is expected to remain stable," it said. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account and the government has pledged to make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  convertible on the capital account, but has not given a timetable. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts expect full convertibility of the yuan could take at least 10 years. \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials say the government is considering making the rigid exchange regime more flexible to cushion trade shocks after it joins the World Trade Organisation, possibly this year or early next year. \par \par \uc2  That may include a wider trade band for the yuan, although officials have not given a timetable or a new range. \par \par \uc2  Over the past three years, the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the yuan within the trade band of 8.2770 and 8.2800, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But since April 2000, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has allowed the yuan to close outside the wafer-thin band 53 times, although only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx5f00409\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
