\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc31}{\*\bkmkend toc31}\b \uc2  China's yuan ends up as local banks sell dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2001\par 07:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 29 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed firmer against the U.S. dollar on Sunday as some local banks sold down surplus dollar positions ahead of a week-long holiday starting Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at an intraday high of 8.2770 to the dollar, compared with Saturday's close of 8.2780. It moved in a narrow range the entire session, touching a low of 8.2778. Turnover rose to $190 million from $140 million on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "Some local banks sold surplus dollars late in the session, pushing up the yuan," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He said local banks offset their positions on Sunday as transactions conducted on Monday, the last trading session ahead of the holiday, would only be settled on May 8. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will close from Tuesday to May 7 for the Labour Day holidays. Trading will resume on May 8. \par \par \uc2  A local bank dealer said domestic banks had nearly completed pre-holiday preparations and trading could be thin with the yuan moving narrowly on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Some banks balanced their positions by selling dollars today and others had bought \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday to prepare for the holiday," he said. "Business is likely to be limited to really urgent needs on Monday." \par \par \uc2  Saturday's local bank dollar buying threatened to make the yuan breach the weak end of its long-standing thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800, but the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity on Sunday and the yuan seemed under control in rangebound trade, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible only under the current account. Local banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-1997, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boxed the yuan in a wafer-thin, 30-notch band partly to deter speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  China has said it will make the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate regime more flexible to cushion trade shocks after it joins the World Trade Organisation, which may occur later this year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been less active on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the past year and allowed the yuan to close outside the band 52 times, although only twice outside its weak end. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has not announced changes in the trade band, although officials have said a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime included a wider band for the yuan. They gave no timetable or range. \par \par \uc2  On Sunday, the yuan weakened to close at 6.6875 against 100 yen from 6.6300 on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  It ended down slightly against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0615 on Sunday against 1.0612 on Friday. There was no trade in Hong Kong dollars on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4t013l3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
