\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc32}{\*\bkmkend toc32}\b \uc2  China's yuan edges down,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  steps in. \b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2001\par 06:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 28 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed down slightly against the U.S. dollar on Saturday after the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in slim trade to prevent the yuan from falling sharply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2780 to the dollar, compared with Friday's close of 8.2774. Turnover was thin at $140 million, down from $170 million. \par \par \uc2  The yuan touched an intraday high of 8.2768 in early trade, but dollar buying ahead of a week-long holiday starting Tuesday quickly pushed it to an intraday low of 8.2797. \par \par \uc2  Continued dollar buying threatened to make the yuan breach the weak end of its long-standing thin trading band at 8.2800 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from falling further, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Domestic banks bought dollars to prepare for possible needs during the holiday, pushing the yuan towards the 8.2800 level," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted to support the yuan and kept the yuan stable around the 8.2780 level late in the session," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to move in a wider range on Sunday and Monday, the last two trading days ahead of the long holiday, but trade was likely to be thin. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will close from Tuesday to May 7 for the International Labour Day holidays. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible only under the current account. Local banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-1997, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boxed the yuan in the wafer-thin band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to deter speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  China has said it will make the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate regime more flexible to cushion trade shocks after it joins the World Trade Organisation, which may occur later this year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been less active on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the past year and allowed the yuan to close outside the band 52 times, although only twice outside its weak end. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has not announced changes in the trade band, although officials have said a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime included a wider band for the yuan. They gave no timetable or range. \par \par \uc2  On Saturday, the yuan gained ground to close at 6.6300 against 100 yen from 6.7006 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  There was no trade in Hong Kong dollars on Saturday. The yuan ended a touch weaker against the Hong Kong dollar to at 1.0612 on Friday versus 1.0611 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4s0166b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
