\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc34}{\*\bkmkend toc34}\b \uc2  China's yuan ends flat in thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2001\par 06:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 20 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed unchanged against the U.S. dollar on Friday as a rough balance of dollar supply and demand in the market led to dull, range-bound trade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2775 to the dollar, unchanged from Thursday's close, after moving in a narrow range of 8.2771 to 8.2799. Turnover remained a small $150 million on Friday, although that was up from Thursday's $100 million. \par \par \uc2  "Banks were less active because there was a rough balance of dollar supply and demand," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Domestic banks usually settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local banks, which account for the bulk of turnover on the market, had plenty of dollars in hand due to China's healthy trade surplus since the start of last year. \par \par \uc2  But the banks had become more reluctant to sell dollars in anticipation of less \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  income, since China's exports were expected to slow this year because of a global economic slowdown, they said. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade firms have to buy and sell most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to domestic banks designated to trade on the market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the yuan to move narrowly around 8.2770 level in the near term. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support or depress the yuan on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in a wafer-thin range of 8.2770 to 8.2800 since mid-1997 partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  It has become less active since April 2000 and allowed the yuan to close outside the band 51 times, although only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2  China has vowed to make its rigid exchange rate regime more flexible to cushion trade shocks after it joins the World Trade Organisation, which may happen late this year. \par \par \uc2  Officials say that includes a wider band for the yuan, but they have given no timetable or range. \par \par \uc2  The yuan weakened slightly to close at 6.7708 against 100 yen on Friday from 6.7668 on Thursday. It ended almost unchanged against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0613 from 1.0614. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4k01qq9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
