\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc36}{\*\bkmkend toc36}\b \uc2  China yuan seen stable amid slowing trade-govt report. \b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2001\par 13:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 17 (Reuters) - China's yuan is expected to remain stable in 2001 despite an anticipated slowdown in exports to the United States and Japan, a Chinese government think tank said in a research report published on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan will be underpinned by a forecast $48 billion inflow in foreign direct investment this year and a narrower gap between yuan and U.S. dollar interest rates, said the State Natural Science Fund Commission. \par \par \uc2  "There is no need to devalue the yuan as foreign investment would grow steadily and interest rate spreads with hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  narrow in 2001," said the report published in the China Securities newspaper. \par \par \uc2  It predicted foreign investment in China to rise about $7.0 billion, and a narrowing dollar/yuan interest spread to help curb \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  capital flight. \par \par \uc2  China's trade surplus was likely to drop "substantially" in 2001 as external demand deteriorates, putting pressure on the yuan which is convertible only on the current account, the report said. \par \par \uc2  It echoed comments from China's Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng, who said last week a slowdown in the U.S. economy would affect China's economic growth this year as would a sluggish Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  China's trade surplus totalled $24.1 billion in 2000, one of the major drivers of its 8.0 percent economic growth last year. Japan was China's biggest trade partner. \par \par \uc2  The weaker Japanese yen would boost China's imports from Japan, while exports could remain stagnant. This would put pressure on the yuan, the think tank said. It gave no figures. \par \par \uc2  Speculation has grown recently that the yen's fall against the dollar could spark off competitive devaluations across Asia. \par \par \uc2  Liu Mingkang, head of the China's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank, the Bank of China, said in early April it would be hard to maintain the yuan's current level against the dollar if the yen fell below 130 to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  China has cut interest rates for one-year dollar deposits three times this year to 3.4375 percent from 5.0 percent following cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve. One-year yuan deposit rates have stayed flat at 2.25 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  STABLE YUAN \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to ensure the yuan trades within a wafer-thin band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to the dollar, holding firm against a devaluation during the 1997-98 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  But since April 2000, the bank seems to be relaxing its grip, allowing the yuan to close outside the tiny range nearly 50 times, though only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2  China has vowed to make its rigid exchange rate regime more flexible to cushion trade shocks expected after it joins the World Trade Organisation. Officials said that includes a wider band for the yuan, although they gave no timetable or range. \par \par \uc2  "China's expected entry to the WTO will cause volatilities in foreign trade and (\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) capital flow, which will then cause uncertainties to the yuan value," the report said. \par \par \uc2  But that will not lead to a devaluation this year, it said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2720 on Tuesday, the highest close since China set up its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 1994 and firmer than the previous record of 8.2761 recorded on February 8. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the unusually strong close was due to a last-minute dollar-selling order and did not represent a new trend. There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, they said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4h023tw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
