\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc37}{\*\bkmkend toc37}\b \uc2  China's yuan edges up as local banks sell dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2001\par 07:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 16 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed a touch firmer against the U.S. dollar in thin trade on Monday due to light dollar selling from local banks on behalf of exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The selling was prompted partly by an official media report that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose sharply in the first three months of the year, an indication of ample dollar supply on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2768 to the dollar, up from Friday's 8.2771 and out of its normal wafer-thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800. It moved in a narrow range of 8.2767 to 8.2773 without signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had $176 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of March, up sharply from $165.6 billion at the end of 2000 and $154.7 billion at the end of 1999, the official China Securities newspaper quoted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong as saying. \par \par \uc2  "Rising \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves indicated ample dollar supply in the market," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "This prompted local banks to sell some dollars today, pushing up the yuan," he said. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. Chinese banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  The central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside its 30-notch bracket since mid-1997, mainly to deter speculation about a yuan devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the bank has also been buying surplus dollars to prevent the yuan from rising too sharply and China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were a barometer of dollar supply on the market, they said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip on the yuan since last year, allowing it to close outside the usual range 49 times, though only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing appears to be testing a wider trade band to cushion against trade shocks after it joins the World Trade Organisation, which is expected late this year. \par \par \uc2  Despite Monday's rise, dealers said they saw limited potential for the yuan to rise further, partly due to expectations of slowing exports growth this year because of a global economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there had been growing reluctance among local banks to sell dollars in anticipation of less \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  income as China's exports slowed. \par \par \uc2  Turnover was slim at only $43 million on Monday, although that was up from a fractional $38 million on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was likely to move narrowly around 8.2770 level in the near term, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan weakened to close at 6.7825 against 100 yen on Monday compared with 6.7290 on Friday. It also weakened against the Hong Kong dollar to end at 1.0614 from 1.0553. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4g021kb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
