\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc38}{\*\bkmkend toc38}\b \uc2  China yuan weakens slightly in very thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2001\par 07:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 13 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed a shade weaker against the U.S. dollar on Friday in extremely sleepy trade ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2771 to the dollar, down from Thursday's 8.2770 as turnover dived to a paltry $38 million from a moderate $160 million. \par \par \uc2  "Very few banks are trading today because the market is usually quite slow ahead of the weekend," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Some foreign bank dealers in Shanghai were on leave on Friday for Easter, which also thinned trade. \par \par \uc2  Banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Despite the yuan's mild weakness in the past two sessions, dealers said dollar sales by local exporters remained active as China's trade surplus showed improving signs last month. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, China said its trade surplus edged up 3.0 percent to $2.37 billion in March, the first annual growth since late 2000. \par \par \uc2  The January-March surplus fell 9.4 percent year on year to $4.73 billion, after a 17.85 percent fall in the first two months of this year. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said China's trade performance was unlikely to extend the March recovery into later this year and there would be pressure on the yuan if exports were depressed by a global economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng said on Thursday a slowdown in the U.S. economy, along with negative factors from the Japan economy, would impact China's economic growth this year. \par \par \uc2  For most of the past three years, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside a bracket between 8.2770 and 8.2800, to deter speculation about a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But in the last year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip on the yuan, allowing it to close outside the usual range 48 times, though only twice outside the weak end of 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing appears to be testing a wider trade band to cushion against trade shocks expected after it joins the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan gained ground against the yen on Friday to close at 6.7290 versus 6.7699. It weakened against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0553 from 1.0511. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4d028j1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
