\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc39}{\*\bkmkend toc39}\b \uc2  China yuan ends a touch down,shrugs off trade data. \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2001\par 07:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 12 (Reuters) - China's yuan inched down one notch on Thursday to end at 8.2770, the firm end of its thin trading band against the U.S. dollar, from Wednesday's 8.2769 on marginally higher dollar demand from importers. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar sales by local exporters remained robust, although the yuan hit an intraday low of 8.2778 when some banks, anxious to fill dollar buying orders, bid prices higher. \par \par \uc2  Turnover climbed to a moderate $160 million, from a sluggish $90 million in the previous session, as the dollar demand picked up, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be no changes in strong dollar supply on the market, though the bid side turns a little active today," said a dealer at an European bank in Shanghai on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the China's release of 24 U.S. aircrew on Wednesday, held for 11 days after their spy plane made an emergency landing in Hainan, had no market impact. \par \par \uc2  News late on Wednesday of a small increase in the trade surplus for March also had little impact as dealers said the improvement was unlikely to continue, and pressure on the yuan could loom as exporters felt the chill of a global slowdown. \par \par \uc2  The trade surplus edged up 3.0 percent to $2.37 billion in March, the first annual growth since late 2000. The January-March surplus fell 9.4 percent year on year to $4.73 billion. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng said on Thursday that a slowdown in the U.S. economy, along with negative factors from the Japanese economy, would impact China's economic growth this year, reinforcing the dealers' caution on the trade data. \par \par \uc2  Banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  The yuan, convertible only on the current account, has remained firm this month as domestic exporters kept offloading their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  incomes to local banks, dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was unlikely to fall through 8.2800 this year as China appeared to be reluctant to give up defending the yuan at the key psychological mark, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  For most of the past three years, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside the band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to deter speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Thursday to pull the yuan back into its thin range. \par \par \uc2  But since April 2000, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip on the yuan, allowing it to close outside the usual range 48 times, though only twice outside the weak end of 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing appeared to be testing waters for a wider trade band to cushion against trade shocks expected after it joins the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The yuan lost further ground against the yen on Thursday to close at 6.7699 versus 6.6770. It strengthened relatively sharply against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0511 from 1.0610. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4c0267k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
