\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc40}{\*\bkmkend toc40}\b \uc2  China yuan firms to end outside usual slim band. \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2001\par 07:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 11 (Reuters) - China's yuan inched up against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday to close outside the firm end of its narrow trading band on abundant dollar sales by local exporters. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2769 to the dollar versus Tuesday's 8.2770 as turnover climbed to a still thin $90 million from a mere $40 million. \par \par \uc2  "The market has become very slow these days because fewer banks are buying dollars, although the supply side is still strong," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  The yuan, convertible only on the current account, has remained firm this month as domestic exporters kept offloading their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to local banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to extend its strength this week, although dollar demand was expected to pick up on anticipated higher imports during the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  For most of the past three years, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside the band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to deter speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Wednesday to cap the yuan's strength. \par \par \uc2  Since April 2000, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip on the yuan, allowing it to close outside the usual range 48 times, though only twice outside the weak end of 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing appeared to be testing waters for a wider trade band to cushion against trade shocks expected after it joins the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan lost ground against the yen to close at 6.6770 versus 6.6305. It edged down marginally against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0610 from 1.0609. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4b02cc8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
