\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc41}{\*\bkmkend toc41}\b \uc2  China yuan strengthens in quiet, thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2001\par 07:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 10 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed stronger on Tuesday at 8.2770, the firm end of its 30-notch trade band against the U.S. dollar, as exporters continued to unload hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues to domestic banks. \par \par \uc2  Turnover contracted sharply to a tiny $40 million from Monday's moderate $180 million as demand for the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from importers dropped, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "It is hard to find a buyer today because only a few banks are bidding dollars," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  For most of the past three years, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside the band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Tuesday to rein in the yuan, which traded outside the strong end of the band for most of the session. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was fairly strong most of last week due to flush dollar supply from exporters, though the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed slightly weaker at 8.2774 on Monday on a bout of late dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have said Monday's brief fall did not point to further weakening this week as Chinese exporters were expected to continue to offload their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account, which consists mainly of trade flows. \par \par \uc2  State media said on Tuesday trade on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had become more active in the January-March period, but it gave no reasons. \par \par \uc2  Daily market turnover rose 78 percent year-on-year in the first quarter to an equivalent $275 million with dollar/yuan trade accounting for 98.85 percent of that, according to official statistics published in the Shanghai Securities News. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said rising foreign trade and actual foreign direct investment might have helped to boost market turnover. Beijing is expected to issue first-quarter trade data later this week. \par \par \uc2  Since April 2000, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip on the yuan, allowing it to close outside the usual range 47 times, though only twice outside the weak end of 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing appeared to be testing the waters for a wider trade band to cushion against trade shocks expected after it joins the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan firmed against the yen to close at 6.6305 versus 6.6400. It edged down against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0609 from 1.0608. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4a02dg0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
