\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc42}{\*\bkmkend toc42}\b \uc2  China yuan weakens, but dealers see strength ahead. \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2001\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 9 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed softer against the U.S. dollar on Monday as dollar-seeking banks came late to the market with yuan offers, after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded fairly strongly for most of the session. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at its intraday low of 8.2774 to the dollar after trading at 8.2769 or 8.2770 throughout most of the session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Turnover declined to a moderate $180 million from a healthy $250 million on Friday when it finished at 8.2766, outside its normal band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Ample dollar sales by local exporters helped the yuan regain strength last week, when it closed outside the strong end of its thin trade band three times. \par \par \uc2  For most of the past three years, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside the band to deter speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Authorities appeared to be more tolerant when the yuan was firming and dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Chinese exporters were expected to continue to unload their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, and Monday's brief fall did not point to further weakening this week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There might not be big drops in dollar supply this week, so the yuan could remain strong," said a dealer at a foreign bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  But other dealers said the yuan could head towards the weak end of 8.2800 later this month as Chinese imports might gain steam in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account, which consists mainly of trade flows. \par \par \uc2  Banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Since April 2000, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing the yuan to close outside its usual range 47 times, though only twice outside the weak end of 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing appeared to be testing waters for a wider trade band to cushion against trade shocks expected after it joins the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yuan weakened against the yen to close at 6.6400 versus 6.6194. It edged up against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0608 from 1.0610. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4902fvt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
