\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc47}{\*\bkmkend toc47}\b \uc2  China central banker - yuan to be firm-BusinessWeek. \b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2001\par 08:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - China's foreign reserves will rise steadily, enabling it to hold its yuan peg to the dollar in the face of the weakening yen, U.S.-based BusinessWeek magazine on Tuesday quoted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Dai Xianglong as saying. \par \par \uc2  China's reserves now total $160 billion and will grow to $200 billion "in the near future", BusinessWeek quoted him as saying. \par \par \uc2  That would allow the People's Bank of China to hold the yuan's hard peg against the dollar even while Asian markets turn choppy, it quoted him as saying. \par \par \uc2  "I can say for sure the Japanese yen will devalue," he said. "This will bring about successive devaluations in Southeast Asian countries, and add to the pressure on the yuan." \par \par \uc2  But he said China had held firm against devaluing during the 1997 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in order to contain regional contagion and would do so again. \par \par \uc2  "The rate will remain stable now and in the early days of our WTO (World Trade Organisation) accession," he was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  In an earlier transcript of the interview released on March 28 by Chinese state media, Dai said he expected a more flexible exchange rate after China joins the WTO. \par \par \uc2  "After WTO entry there will be bigger changes in the balance of payments along with changes in foreign trade and investment," the Financial News quoted Dai as saying. \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rate will have some flexibility," Dai said. "But generally speaking we will continue to implement and improve the current exchange system." \par \par \uc2  Dai said China's economy was now sound and "the yuan exchange rate is stable and has a solid foundation". \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to ensure the yuan trades within a wafer-thin band, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trapped in a narrow band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 to the dollar for more than three years after the Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  But the bank seems to be relaxing its grip; it has allowed the yuan to close outside the hair-thin range more than 40 times since April 2000, though only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2  It ended steady at 8.2775 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible only on the current account, which mainly covers trade and services, and is now well supported by China's healthy balance of payments surplus. \par \par \uc2  Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less likely to give up defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the psychological 8.2800 mark, at least this year, to deter speculation of a devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4302nk3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
