\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc48}{\*\bkmkend toc48}\b \uc2  China's SAFE sees more flexible  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  rules-media. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tiffany Wu \par \uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2001\par 12:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 3 (Reuters) - China may allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, to move in a wider daily range of up to 0.3 percent up or down to make its rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime more flexible, state media on Tuesday quoted a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator as saying. \par \par \uc2  The deputy director of the balance of payments department of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE), Zou Lin, also said China would soon allow foreign banks to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to non-foreign funded enterprises, state media reported. \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials have said the country would allow a wider trading band for the yuan to cushion against external trade shocks expected after it joins the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  But they have not given a range nor timetable. \par \par \uc2  State media also did not give a timeframe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Zou was not immediately available for comment. \par \par \uc2  "For now, China's main task is to keep a stable exchange rate. At the same time, we need to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float up to its 0.3 percent daily trade limit," the semi-official China News Agency on Tuesday quoted Zou as saying at a Beijing conference. \par \par \uc2  "This would help the (Chinese) markets gradually adapt to exchange rate volatility," Zou said. \par \par \uc2  Regulations on the Shanghai-based \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market set up in 1994 already allow the yuan to move up to 0.3 percent higher or lower than the weighted average price logged in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  But for most of the past three years, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside 8.2770 to 8.2800 to the dollar, to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Although a 0.3 percent range would still be narrow, it would be significantly wider than the current band, equivalent to 0.018 percent up or down from the mid point. \par \par \uc2  According to the China News Agency, Zou said the SAFE also planned to relax restrictions on how much \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  a bank can hold against yuan assets, but gave no details. \par \par \uc2  FOREIGN BANKS ALLOWED MORE ACCESS \par \par \uc2  At the same conference, Zou said a new draft of rules governing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading for foreign banks in China had been completed and a formal version would be issued "very soon", the China Daily reported. \par \par \uc2  Under the new regulations, foreign banks in China would be allowed to trade \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with non-foreign funded companies, putting them on equal footing with domestic banks, the paper quoted Zhou as saying, but did not give a time frame. \par \par \uc2  Under existing rules, foreign banks are not allowed to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  business with Chinese firms, although some have circumvented regulations by issuing letters of credit to local firms, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  China has pledged to allow foreign banks to conduct yuan business with Chinese firms two years after it enters the WTO, and with Chinese individuals five years after accession. \par \par \uc2  "The establishment of a unified system for all banks authorised to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlement and payment services in China is in response to the changes that will come when China joins the WTO," Zou said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said it was not clear whether his comments represented a speeding up of this timetable. \par \par \uc2  "The key is whether they will let foreign banks take yuan deposits from local firms. If so, this will be a huge boost to our business," said a European bank treasury official in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said some foreign banks dodged regulations by issuing letters of credit (LCs) to domestic companies. \par \par \uc2  "This is quite widespread. Regulators don't seem to crack down on LCs now," said a banker at another European bank. \par \par \uc2  "If Zou means new regulations legalise LCs, then the impact on foreign banks' business would not be as great as if China allowed us to take yuan deposits," the banker said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4302nbg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
