\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc50}{\*\bkmkend toc50}\b \uc2  China sees more flexible exchange rate after WTO. \b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 03:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, March 28 (Reuters) - China will adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate after its entry to the World Trade Organisation (WTO), \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong was quoted as saying on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "After WTO entry there will be bigger changes in the balance of payments along with changes in foreign trade and investment," the Financial News quoted Dai Xianglong as saying in an interview with U.S. based magazine BusinessWeek. Talks on China's WTO entry are likely to drag on at least until late this year. \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rate will have some flexibility," Dai said. "But generally speaking we will continue to implement and improve the current exchange system," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dai said China's economy was now sound and "the yuan exchange rate is stable and has a solid foundation". \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  now \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to ensure the yuan trades within a wafer thin band. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed on Tuesday at 8.2774 per dollar compared with Monday's close of 8.2769 after moving in a narrow range of 8.2769 to 8.2775. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3s034dy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
