\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc53}{\*\bkmkend toc53}\b \uc2  China yuan edges up in thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 07:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, March 26 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed a notch firmer against the U.S. dollar on Monday due to light dollar selling by local banks late in the session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Trade was extremely thin as expectations of China's slowing exports this year made local banks reluctant to sell more dollars on hand, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2769, compared with Friday's close of 8.2770 after moving in a narrow range of 8.2768 to 8.2773. \par \par \uc2  The close was outside of the strong end of the yuan's normal thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  enforced through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for about three years, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But dealers said there was no evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "There was a rough balance of dollar supply and demand on the market, keeping the yuan in a narrow range," said a local bank deal. "It was light dollar selling by local banks in the last few minutes of trading that pushed the yuan up a notch." \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks accumulated heavy dollar positions last year when China scored a healthy foreign trade surplus, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Exporters must sell most of their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to banks, while importers are allowed to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on required trade-related documents. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, turnover was only $40 million, down sharply from an already moderate $200 million on Friday, according to figures published on the market's website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Turnover has dropped significantly since the start of March as China's foreign trade surplus was falling, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Average daily turnover has fallen to around $180 million in March from $402 million in February, exchange figures showed. \par \par \uc2  China's foreign trade surplus, which fell 17.85 percent year-on-year to $2.37 billion in the first two months of this year, is key for dollar supply and demand on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because the yuan is only convertible under the current account. \par \par \uc2  Top Chinese officials have warned weak global economies would likely to pressure China's exports, leading to slowing foreign trade and dropping trade surplus in 2001 compared with 2000. \par \par \uc2  "Local banks have become more reluctant to sell dollars on hand due to expectations of slowing exports for China this year," said a dealer at an European Bank. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the yuan to move narrowly around the current level in thin trade in the near term," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was now at high levels and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed less inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to affect its movements. \par \par \uc2  The bank has reduced its activity in the past year, allowing the yuan to close outside the usual range 44 times since April 2000, but only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing is moving towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to cushion China from trade shocks after its entry to the World Trade Organisation, which is expected this year. \par \par \uc2  But Beijing was less likely to give up defending the yuan at the key psychological 8.2800 mark, at least this year, to deter speculation of a yuan devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yuan strengthened to close at 6.6928 against 100 yen from 6.7050 on Friday. It also firmed against the Hong Kong dollar to end at 1.0612 from 1.0618. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3q03i1j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
