\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc54}{\*\bkmkend toc54}\b \uc2  China yuan ends flat, strength expected next week. \b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 05:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, March 23 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed unchanged at 8.2770 against the U.S. dollar on Friday, the third session in a row as dollar sales by domestic exporters persisted. \par \par \uc2  The yuan touched a intraday high of 8.2768 and low of 8.2778 before closing at the firm end of its long-standing 30-notch trade band. \par \par \uc2  Turnover rose to a moderate $200 million from Thursday's $170 million as dollar demand picked up slightly. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was expected to hold onto its firmness next week as China's trade surplus for the January-February period would keep dollars flowing into the national \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks typically settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the Shanghai-based market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Exporters must sell most of their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to banks, while importers are allowed to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on required trade-related documents. \par \par \uc2  "There seemed to be no drops in dollar supply, although the demand side grew slightly today," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has largely been solid so far this month as China's trade surplus rose to $2.37 billion for the first two months of 2001 from $600 million in December and $520 million in November. \par \par \uc2  But the yuan was likely to come under pressure later this year if a projected global economic slowdown pulled China's trade balance into a deficit for one or even a few months, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Under a rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, the yuan traded in a hair-thin range of 8.2770 to 8.2800, enforced by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for about three years. \par \par \uc2  But the bank reduced its activity in the past year, allowing the yuan to close outside the usual range more than 40 times since April 2000, although only twice outside the weak end. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Friday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing is moving towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to cushion China from trade shocks after its entry to the World Trade Organisation, probably this year. \par \par \uc2  But Beijing was unlikely to give up defending the yuan at the key psychological 8.2800 mark, at least this year, to deter speculation of a yuan devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan weakened against the yen to close at 6.7050 versus Thursday's 6.7015. It also softened against the Hong Kong dollar to end at 1.0618 from 1.0615. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3n03hv0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
