\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc60}{\*\bkmkend toc60}\b \uc2  China's yuan ends unchanged after c. bank support. \b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 06:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, March 2 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed unchanged against the U.S. dollar on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan against pressure from the opening of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B share trading to locals, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2780 to the dollar, unchanged from Thursday. It hit an intraday high of 8.2778 and a low of 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  "Banks have seen unexpectedly strong demand for shifts in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits from individual accounts to securities houses for B share trading," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Some banks have reported tens of millions of dollars being transferred for a single bank," he said. "Although much of the money has yet to flow into B shares, most local banks have been reluctant to sell dollars and some even bought some." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the yuan touched the intraday low of 8.2800 late in the session after falling for most of it. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to want to signal the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that it will not allow the yuan to fall below the weak end of its normal trading band," another local bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Turnover rose to $230 million on Friday, up from a $220 million on Thursday, according to figures published by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  CONCERN OVER B SHARE REFORM \par \par \uc2  Bank dealers said the impact of opening B shares to local investors seemed to have been bigger than expected and worries over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflow was increasing. \par \par \uc2  "The momentum of shifting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for B share trading now seems beyond expectations," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  "It is affecting bank business," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said bankers were concerned by media reports of the B share frenzy and on the number of B share trading accounts being opened. \par \par \uc2  State media said 340,000 new accounts were opened on Monday and Tuesday, but have not updated the figure since. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. Banks typically adjust \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their corporate clients, mostly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the possibility of an outflow of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from bank savings to the B share markets had weighed on the yuan trade over the past few sessions. \par \par \uc2  China allowed citizens with legal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank savings would be allowed to trade B shares, previously reserved for foreigners, from Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The decision freed up as much as $75 billion in personal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits for investment in the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, although banks had expected only a limited outflow of some $2.0 billion. \par \par \uc2  OUTFLOW COULD GROW \par \par \uc2  That figure could now be larger depending on the expansion of capitalisation in the B share market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The markets had swelled from $7.2 billion in capitalisation before B share reform to $9.6 billion on Friday. \par \par \uc2  B shares resumed trading on Wednesday after a week-long halt and surged nearly their 10 percent daily limit for three straight days without real turnover due to a lack of sellers. \par \par \uc2  Over the past three years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in a wafer-thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800, partly to quell speculation of a possible devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The bank has reduced its activity and allowed the yuan to close outside the range more than 40 times since April 2000, but only twice out of the weak end of 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing is testing the waters for a wider trading band to cushion China from trade shocks after its entry to the World Trade Organisation, probably this year. But they say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears reluctant to allow the yuan to weaken. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan strengthened to end at 6.9454 to 100 Japanese yen from 7.0452 on Thursday. It weakened to close at 1.0619 to the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0610. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3205kqp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
