\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc62}{\*\bkmkend toc62}\b \uc2  China's yuan ends up as banks trim dollar holdings. \b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2001\par 06:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Feb 16 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against the U.S. dollar on Friday as local banks sold surplus dollars they had settled with exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2768 to the dollar, up from 8.2774 on Thursday. It moved in a narrow range of 8.2766 to 8.2771. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In spite of a decline on Thursday, the yuan would generally continue its strong trend of the past few weeks due to oversupply of dollars on the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Domestic banks have a lot of dollars on their hands as they have settled a load of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with exporters over the past few weeks," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The banks sold surplus dollars on the market today, pushing up the yuan again after a break on Thursday," he said. \par \par \uc2  China's trade firms are required to sell most of their dollar earnings to domestic banks, which later settle them on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to deciding dollar supply and demand on the market as the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Backed by China's healthy foreign trade surplus, the yuan spent most of the week moving near 8.2761, the highest close since China set up the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and unifed its dual exchange systems in 1994. \par \par \uc2  Official figures published on Wednesday showed China recorded a trade surplus of $1.375 billion in January. \par \par \uc2  Although the surplus was down from $1.5 billion a year earlier, dealers said it still indicated a strong inflow of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, turnover on the market remained extremely thin at only $60 million, down from a slim $80 million on Thursday, according the market's website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They said the yuan was likely to move narrowly around the 8.2770 level next week. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has enforced a wafer-thin trading range of 8.2770 to 8.2800 for the past three years, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But the bank has relaxed its rigid control over the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, allowing the yuan to close outside that thin band 41 times since April 2000. Analysts said the bank was quietly testing the waters for a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials say they are considering a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime but have given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll in December showed most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the yuan to flow in a range of five percent up or down by end-2001, compared with a fraction of less than one percent at present. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan firmed slightly against the Japanese yen to end at 7.1556 versus 7.1571 on Thursday. It also strengthened to 1.0612 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0660. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx2g06nbb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
