\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc65}{\*\bkmkend toc65}\b \uc2  China's yuan ends near record high, weakness seen. \b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2001\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Feb 14 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday, approaching a historical high close as exporters continued to sell dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the yuan was likely to lose steam and ease in the near term as few bank traders wanted to buy dollars at present high levels, resulting in thin trade, they said. \par \par \uc2  Turnover on the Shanghai-based market plunged to just $50 million from a heavy $430 million on Tuesday, according to the market's website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2763 to the dollar, up from 8.2766 on Tuesday and brushing near 8.2761, the highest close since 1994 when China set up the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and unifed its dual exchange systems. That high was hit last Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It moved in a range of 8.2756 and 8.2766 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Exporters continued to sell dollars on the market today, pushing up the yuan," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But dollar buying abated significantly due to the yuan's high exchange rates. Bank traders only bought for urgent needs," he said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was expected to fall in the near term, though slightly, he said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has been strong since last year, helped by a steady supply of dollars fuelled by China's healthy trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. Banks usually adjust their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the national market after trading the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly exporters and importers. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has enforced a wafer-thin trading range of 8.2770 to 8.2800 for the past three years, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But the bank has relaxed its rigid control over the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, allowing the yuan to close outside its normal thin band 40 times since April 2000, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have quietly set a new strong end for the yuan's movements, possibly at 8.2760, as it tests the waters for a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials say they are considering a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to cushion trade shocks related to entry to the World Trade Organisation, expected this year. They have given no timetable for a wider trading band. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll in December showed most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the yuan to flow in a range of five percent up or down by the end of 2001, compared with a fraction of less than one percent at present. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan weakened against the Japanese yen to close at 7.0718 versus 7.0481 on Tuesday. It strengthened to end at 1.0608 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0614. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx2e06hd4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
