\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc70}{\*\bkmkend toc70}\b \uc2  China's yuan slips outside weak end of trade band. \b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2001\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Jan 23 (Reuters) - China's yuan slipped to 8.2810 against the U.S. dollar in slim volume on Tuesday, closing outside the weak end of its long-standing band for the first time since April 2000. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the close offered a clear signal that China was relaxing its iron grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has for years kept the yuan boxed in a razor-thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800. The last time it ended outside the weak end was on April 12, when it hit a 28-month low of 8.2830. \par \par \uc2  "There is so little trade today and so yuan levels are seen to be more volatile," said a dealer with a foreign bank in Shanghai. "We didn't see any signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity." \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering widening the trading band on the yuan from its current range of a fraction of one percent to help cope with trade shocks related to its entry to the World Trade Organisation. China expects to join the global trade group in the next few months. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com}}}\uc2  reported sluggish volume of $100 million on Tuesday compared to the previous session's $360 million. Trading ranged between 8.2770 and 8.2820. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has ended outside of its slim band more than 30 times since last April, but Tuesday's close was only the second time outside the weak end of the band. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been inclined to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the weak end of the band to stamp out speculation of a yuan devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said Tuesday's close indicated no clear trend for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since trading ahead of the Chinese New Year holidays, which begin on Wednesday, has been volatile. \par \par \uc2  At the weekend, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed twice at 8.2762 against the U.S. dollar, its highest closes since China unified its dual exchange rate system in 1994, on strong demand for the yuan to meet a surge in spending ahead of the holidays. \par \par \uc2  The yuan strengthened against the Japanese yen on Tuesday to close at 7.0720 against 7.0800 on Monday. There was no trade in the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  China's national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be closed from January 24 to 30 for the Lunar New Year holidays. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1n0710t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
