\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc71}{\*\bkmkend toc71}\b \uc2  UPDATE 2-China's yuan slides outside weak end of trade band. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lee Chyen Yee \par \uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2001\par 08:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Jan 23 (Reuters) - China's yuan slipped to 8.2810 against the U.S. dollar in slim volume on Tuesday, closing outside the weak end of its long-standing band for the first time since April 2000. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the close offered another clear signal that China was relaxing its iron grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept the yuan boxed in a razor-thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 for years. The last time it ended outside the weak end was on April 12, when it hit a 28-month low of 8.2830. \par \par \uc2  "There is so little trade today and so yuan levels are seen to be more volatile," said a dealer with a foreign bank in Shanghai. "We didn't see any signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity." \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering widening the yuan's trading band from its current range of a fraction of one percent to help cope with trade shocks related to its entry to the World Trade Organisation, which China expects to join this year. \par \par \uc2  China has offered repeated pledges of a move towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime - the latest from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Dai Xianglong earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  "They will continue to allow it to trade outside the current band in the lead-up to officially widening the band," said Paul Schymyck, regional economist at IDEAglobal.com in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts in December forecast China would let the yuan trade up or down up to five percent by the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  In December, two \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  researchers suggested in a report that the yuan could trade in a band of plus or minus 10 to 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  TWICE OUTSIDE WEAK END \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com}}}\uc2  reported sluggish volume of $100 million on Tuesday compared to the Monday's $360 million. Trading ranged between 8.2770 and 8.2820. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has ended outside its slim band more than 30 times since last April, but Tuesday's close was only the second time outside the weak end of the band. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been inclined to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the weak end of the band to stamp out speculation of a yuan devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was kept firm by China's strong trade surplus, which was $24.1 billion in 2000, down from $29.1 billion in 1999. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the surplus was likely to narrow with exports easing due to a slowdown in the U.S. economy and rising imports following WTO membership. \par \par \uc2  "We are expecting slowing exports because of the strong renminbi (yuan) and the slowing down of the U.S. economy," said Chan Yuk Keung, investment strategist at the CEF Brokerage in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible only on the current account and banks usually settle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the national market after trading with corporate clients, mostly exporters and importers. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Tuesday's close indicated no clear trend for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since trading ahead of the Chinese New Year holidays, which begin on Wednesday, has been volatile. \par \par \uc2  "Trade is pretty quiet with only very few people in the market. That's why the yuan has fluctuated more these few days," said a dealer with a domestic bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  At the weekend, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed twice at 8.2762 against the U.S. dollar, its highest closes since China unified its dual exchange rate system in 1994, on strong demand for the yuan to meet a surge in spending ahead of the holidays. \par \par \uc2  The yuan strengthened against the Japanese yen on Tuesday to close at 7.0720 against 7.0800 on Monday. There was no trade in the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  China's national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be closed from January 24 to 30 for the Lunar New Year holidays. \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \tab \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1n06vu9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
