\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc72}{\*\bkmkend toc72}\b \uc2  Pre-holiday demand keeps China's yuan strong. \b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2001\par 06:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Jan 16 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed unchanged and remained strong against the U.S. dollar in active trade on Tuesday due to an increased yuan demand ahead of a long Chinese Lunar New Year holiday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2766 to the dollar, unchanged from Monday, after moving between 8.2765 and 8.2768, spending the entire session outside its usual thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Turnover was also unchanged and heavy at $350 million, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com}}}\uc2 , the website of the Shanghai-based market, the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System. \par \par \uc2  "Yuan buying by local banks ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday continued dominated the market," said a dealer at a U.S. banker. "The yuan's strength is likely to persist until the break." \par \par \uc2  The exchange will be closed from January 24 to 30 for the Lunar New Year holiday, which usually spurs a spending spree in China. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no evidence the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the yuan's strength on Tuesday. The bank usually keeps to the sidelines in the Lunar New Year period, when a rise did not represent a trend, they said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boxed the yuan in a band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for three years. \par \par \uc2  But since April, the yuan has closed outside the strong end of the band 29 times. Analysts said Beijing was testing a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to offset trade shocks expected from its membership to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  China expects to join the world trade club this year and the government has said it is considering making the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible. But it has given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll in December found most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts expected China to widen the trading range to five percent up or down by end-2001, compared to the current fraction of one percent. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. Banks usually adjust \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mainly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  The yuan weakened slightly against the yen to close at 6.9949 from 6.9364 on Monday. It ended little changed at 1.0607 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0608. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1g00nno\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
