\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc73}{\*\bkmkend toc73}\b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Higher On Exporters' Dlr Sales; Bonds Up \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2001\par 10:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China's yuan ended stronger against the U.S. dollar at its intraday high of CNY8.2768 Wednesday, from Tuesday's CNY8.2770, on dollar selling by exporters, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Government bonds settled higher as investors bought into issues ahead of the two-week Chinese New Year holiday, which starts Jan. 22. \par \par \uc2  The dollar reached a session high of CNY8.2772. A total of US$249 million dollars changed hands, down from US$261 million Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar supply continues to be ample," said a trader at Rabobank in Shanghai. Under Chinese law, most exporters are required to repatriate 85% of their overseas receipts to the state-owned banks. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which always keeps a close eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the half-day session, although the dollar closed slightly outside the implicit CNY8.2770-CNY8.2800 band it is normally kept within, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar dipped to CNY1.0607 from CNY1.0616. The Japanese yen fell to CNY7.0803 per Y100 from CNY7.1307. \par \par \uc2  The fixed-income market staged a solid performance in Wednesday's afternoon session, with all seven bonds posting sharp gains. \par \par \uc2  "Investors were adding bonds to their portfolios head of the long holiday," said a trader at Shenyin & Wanguo Securities in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  Investors prefer to hold bonds over the two-week Chinese New Year break, rather than hold shares which are perceived as more high-risk, he explained. \par \par \uc2  The 1999 10-year bond soared CNY0.52 to CNY100.60 on trade of 654,493 lots. Its yield slipped to 3.35% from 3.42%. \par \par \uc2  The 1999 eight-year bond gained CNY0.40 to CNY100.55 on trade of 189,713 lots. Its yield slid to 3.40% from 3.47%. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume totaled CNY1.26 billion, up from CNY1.03 billion Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  China's bond market is off-limits to foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218-3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx1a005qf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
