\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc79}{\*\bkmkend toc79}\b \uc2  POLL-Yuan band no wider than +/-5 pct end 2001. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Edwin Chan \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2000\par 05:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Dec 27 (Reuters) - China is expected to widen the trading range for the yuan to no more than five percent up or down by end-2001, according to a Reuters poll, as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts scale back their estimates for the key reform. \par \par \uc2  Due to slow official progress towards a wider band, forecasts offered by 10 of the 13 respondents were narrower than those in a July poll, in which most experts forecast a band of plus or minus five percent or more in the same time frame. \par \par \uc2  Three gave no forecast in the latest poll. \par \par \uc2  Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts expect China to broaden the range from its current de facto band of a fraction of one percent to cushion trade shocks expected after joining the World Trade Organisation. China expects to enter WTO in the next few months. \par \par \uc2  Five of the 10 respondents to the band question in the latest poll said the range would be three to five percent, while five others reckoned on a range of less than three percent. \par \par \uc2  The lowest estimate was for a tiny 0.15 percent. \par \par \uc2  For three years, the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the market to prevent the yuan from closing outside a range of 8.2770 to 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Taking 8.2785 as a mid-point, that represents a range of just 0.018 percent up or down. A five percent band would allow the yuan to trade between 7.8646 and 8.6924. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a lack of any official announcement to widen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band had led them to pare back forecasts, though 10 of the 13 analysts polled felt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would make some move by the end of next year. Three gave no estimate. \par \par \uc2  Since April 12, the yuan has closed outside its usual band 25 times and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  less frequently. \par \par \uc2  All but one of those closes were outside the strong end of the band, including Tuesday when it ended at 8.2768. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng said in October it was up to the market to determine the right time for widening the band but gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that despite the unusual closes, yuan movements have been minor - only a few notches outside the old range. \par \par \uc2  "This could indicate the government could still be quite cautious about widening the band and that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms would be carried out at a very gradual pace," said professor Xi Junyang at Shanghai University of Finance and Economics. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said China's fragile financial sector was far from ready for full convertibility, a prospect 11 of the 13 respondents saw as at least five years off. The yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  China has kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a tight leash since the 1997 Asia financial crisis, and would take only slow, gradual steps towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "As the Asian experience showed, if you move ahead too eagerly, it could backfire. You have to proceed in a very cautious manner," said Guonan Ma, China economist with Merrill Lynch in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts say the yuan convertibility timeline is linked to China's WTO entry, but Beijing needs to strengthen its troubled banking sector and improve macroeconomic management. \par \par \uc2  "This will of course be tied in partly with the WTO process and the inevitable changes in the banking sector governed by WTO-related changes over the next three to five years," said David Simmonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Citibank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Under a Sino-U.S. WTO agreement, China will lift geographic and customer restrictions on foreign banks within five years from entry, forcing domestic banks to compete with foreign rivals on a level playing field. \par \par \uc2  Band \par \par \uc2  Convertibility \par \par \uc2  (+/-, pct)     (years) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Citibank-SSB\tab \tab \tab \tab      3 - 5\tab     -\par Development Research Centre\tab \tab   0.15\tab      -\par Bank of America\tab \tab \tab \tab   3 - 5\tab     5\par Beijing Tianze Economics Institution     3 - 5\tab     5\par BNP Prime Peregrine\tab \tab \tab \tab 1\tab \tab 5\par IDEAglobal.com\tab \tab \tab \tab      3\tab \tab 5+\par Merrill Lynch\tab \tab \tab \tab    2.0-2.5\tab    5+\par Nomura International\tab \tab \tab    2 - 3\tab     5\par Shanghai Univ of Finance and Economics   1 - 2\tab     5\par Standard Chartered\tab \tab \tab \tab  5\tab      10\par Bank of China Inst of Int Finance\tab     -\tab      5-6\par China Int'l Capital Corp\tab \tab \tab  -\tab \tab 5\par Shanghai Academy of Social Sciences\tab   -\tab     5-10\par -------\tab  ------\par December poll average\tab \tab \tab    2.7\tab     5.7\par July poll average\tab \tab \tab \tab  4.7\tab     6.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Note: Averages computed using midpoint of any ranges\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (With additional reporting by Brian Rhoads).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwcr097bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
