\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc84}{\*\bkmkend toc84}\b \uc2  China yuan weakens after late dollar buying spree. \b0\par\par\uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2000\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Dec 11 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed softer against the U.S. dollar on Monday as late dollar buying by domestic importers pulled down the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2792 to one dollar, compared with 8.2768 in the previous session, after moving in an intraday range between 8.2769 and 8.2792. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which typically pushes the yuan back into its usual tiny band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Turnover rose to a moderate $120 million from a paltry $30 million on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan was stable around the 8.2770 in early trade, but some orders came in late and banks had to buy dollars at higher cost in order to get their deals done by today," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Banks usually settle their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mostly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible only on the current account, and its value is mainly driven by the country's trade performance. \par \par \uc2  Since late November, the yuan has drifted towards the strong end of its long-standing band due to rising dollar sales as local exporters shipped more goods to meet Christmas and New Year demand, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Exporters started to wrap up their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, preparing for book-closing, which also helped drive up yuan demand, they added. \par \par \uc2  China's exports rose 30.1 percent year on year to $227.2 billion in the first 11 months of this year, state television reported on Sunday, quoting Premier Zhu Rongji. \par \par \uc2  Zhu revealed the January-November export figures in a lecture to senior economic officials at a conference on combating export document fraud, the television report said but gave no corresponding imports figure. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has capped the yuan within the wafer-thin band for nearly three years, but it has allowed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end outside the strong end of its normal band about 20 times since May. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the bank was moving towards a wider band as China drew close to membership in the World Trade Organisation, expected in the next few months. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has vowed to pursue a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to prepare for WTO entry, which expected to have a huge impact on the country's trade performance. But officials have given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  While some analysts expected China's WTO accession might put pressure on the yuan due to surging imports, others are optimistic, at least for the short-term, citing the country's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were $161.3 billion at the end of October, the official China Trade News said on Saturday, quoting the Ministry of Foreign Trade. The end-September figure was $160.1 billion, official figures showed. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yuan ended stronger at 7.4540 against the yen, compared with 7.4701 on Friday. It closed at 1.0610 against the Hong Kong dollar, softening from 1.0607. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwcb0a4at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pi