\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc90}{\*\bkmkend toc90}\b \uc2  China's yuan strengthens on exporter dollar sales. \b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2000\par 06:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Dec 1 (Reuters) - China's yuan gained ground against the U.S. dollar on Friday as domestic exporters continued to sell dollar revenues to banks. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2772 to the dollar, compared with 8.2797 on Thursday, after hovering in an intraday range of 8.2768 and 8.2779. \par \par \uc2  For nearly three years, the yuan has been boxed within a razor-thin band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar sales were quite strong today, lifting the yuan almost throughout the session," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The value of the yuan is mainly driven by China's trade performance as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Banks settle \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients, mostly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say the yuan usually firms towards the end of the year as Chinese exporters ship more goods overseas to meet Christmas and New Year demand and have more dollars to sell. \par \par \uc2  Some expected the yuan to strengthen to the 8.2770 level next week, the strong end of its usual tight trading band. \par \par \uc2  But others said the yuan might slip as China's economic growth was gaining steam and this would boost imports. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have reduced its activity, allowing the yuan to end outside the strong end of its normal band nearly 20 times. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say it is testing waters for a wider band as China draws close to membership in the World Trade Organisation, which would affect China's trade performance. \par \par \uc2  Officials have pledged to pursue gradually a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to prepare for China's WTO membership, but have given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to step into the market next week unless rising imports pushed the yuan outside the 8.2800 mark. \par \par \uc2  The market might speculate on a yuan devaluation if Beijing did not defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the weaker end of its usual band, they said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan fell against the yen to close at 7.5047 from Thursday's 7.4410. It also weakened against the Hong Kong dollar to end at 1.0610, compared with 1.0609 in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwc10bjnk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
