\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc92}{\*\bkmkend toc92}\b \uc2  China yuan ends firmer on exporters' dollar sales. \b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2000\par 05:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 29 (Reuters) - China's yuan strengthened against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday as domestic exporters continued to sell the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2775, compared to 8.2800 on Tuesday, after moving in a tight range between 8.2773 and 8.2782. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar sales were still pretty strong today, though demand for dollars was also high compared to early this month," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The value of the yuan is driven mainly by China's trade performance as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only convertible on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Banks settle their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions against the yuan on the national market after trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their corporate clients, mostly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the yuan lost its month-long strength and bank traders reported fresh dollar demand from trade firms, a sign that China's imports were picking up. \par \par \uc2  But dealers say they expect dollar sales to remain active until the end of the year as Chinese exporters usually ship more goods overseas to meet Christmas and New Year demand. \par \par \uc2  The central People's Bank of China has sandwiched the yuan within a wafer-thin band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 for nearly three years, but dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-April, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have reduced its activity, allowing the yuan to end outside its normal band nearly 20 times. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Beijing is testing the waters for a wider band as China draws close to membership in the World Trade Organisation, which would affect the country's trade performance. \par \par \uc2  Officials have pledged to pursue gradually a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to prepare for China's WTO membership, but have given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan softened against the yen to close at 7.5168, compared with 7.4970 on Tuesday. It also ended weaker against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0612 from 1.0608 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbt003sp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
