\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc93}{\*\bkmkend toc93}\b \uc2  China's yuan edges down in thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2000\par 05:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 24 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended a touch weaker against the U.S. dollar in thin trade on Friday due to light dollar buying by local importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2780 to the dollar, down from Thursday's 8.2777. It moved in a narrow range between 8.2774 and 8.2783. \par \par \uc2  "Slight dollar buying from local banks on behalf of importers pushed the yuan slightly down in typical thin trade ahead of the weekend," said a local bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade firms are required to settle their dollar/yuan positions through local banks designated to trade on the national market, China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has weakened slightly this week as bank traders reported fresh dollar demand from trade firms, a sign that China's imports were picking up. \par \par \uc2  It moved mainly in a range of 8.2780 and 8.2790 against a range of 8.2765 and 8.2770 over the previous two weeks. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said dollar selling pressure was also heavy as the fourth quarter was typically a peak period for Chinese exports due to rising overseas demand for the Christmas period. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to dollar supply and demand on the market as the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rough balance between dollar supply and demand was likely to keep the yuan in a narrow range between 8.2780 and 8.2790 next week. \par \par \uc2  They said there were no signs of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity in the market this week. \par \par \uc2  The central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in a thin trade band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 over the past three years, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the bank had reduced its activity over the past few months, allowing the yuan to end outside the wafer-thin range nearly 20 times since Mid-April. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the bank is testing waters for a wider band as China draws close to membership in the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  Officials have pledged to pursue a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime gradually to prepare for China's WTO membership, but has given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan strengthened against the Japanese yen to close at 7.4565, up from 7.5239 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It ended marginally stronger against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0606 compared with 1.0608 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbo00xbg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
