\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc94}{\*\bkmkend toc94}\b \uc2  China's yuan edges up in active trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2000\par 06:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 23 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended a touch firmer against the U.S. dollar on Thursday in heavy trade, with active dollar buying and selling on demand from trade firms, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at an intraday high of 8.2777 to the dollar, up from Wednesday's 8.2780. It touched an intraday low of 8.2784. \par \par \uc2  Turnover nearly doubled to $240 million from $123 million, according to figures published by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com}}}\uc2 , the website of the Shanghai-based national market, the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System. \par \par \uc2  "Both dollar buying and selling from domestic banks on behalf of trade companies were strong," said a local bank trader. \par \par \uc2  "This indicates healthy foreign trade," he said. \par \par \uc2  Another dealer said the yuan had stood at 8.2780 for most of the day. It was only late in the session that dollar selling gained a slight upper hand and pushed the yuan to close at the intraday high. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, the yuan weakened slightly as bank traders reported fresh dollar demand from trade firms, a sign that China's imports were picking up. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said dollar selling pressure was also heavy as the fourth quarter was typically a peak period for Chinese exports due to increased overseas demand for the Christmas period. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade firms are required to settle their dollar/yuan positions through local banks designated to trade on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Trade is key to dollar supply and demand on the market as the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to move narrowly around the 8.2780 level in the near term. \par \par \uc2  They said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. \par \par \uc2  The bank has acted to keep the yuan in a thin trade band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 over the past three years, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced its activity over the past few months, allowing the yuan to end outside the wafer-thin range nearly 20 times since Mid-April. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the bank was testing the waters for a gradual widening of the band as China draws close to membership in the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The government has pledged to pursue a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime gradually to prepare for China's accession to the global trade body, but has given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan weakened against the Japanese yen to close at 7.5239, down from 7.3775 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  It ended marginally weaker against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0608 compared with 1.0606 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbn01030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
