\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc96}{\*\bkmkend toc96}\b \uc2  China's yuan firms as importer dollar buying eases. \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2000\par 05:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 22 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended firmer against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday as local banks bought fewer dollars on behalf of importers. \par \par \uc2  After weakening in the previous two sessions, the yuan closed at an intraday high of 8.2780 to the dollar, up from Tuesday's 8.2785. It touched an intraday low of 8.2786. \par \par \uc2  Turnover dropped sharply to $123 million from $170 million, according to figures published by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com}}}\uc2 , the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System's website. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar buying from local banks for importers eased after the yuan fell over the past two days," said one local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The easing was reflected in reduced turnover and this allowed the yuan to take a break from its downward trend," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were signs China's imports were picking up, creating fresh dollar demand from trade firms and helping push the yuan down slightly this week. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade firms are required to settle their dollar/yuan positions through local banks designated to trade on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Trade is key to dollar supply and demand on the market as the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to move narrowly in a range of 8.2780 and 8.2790 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Before this week's fall, the yuan had risen to trade mainly between 8.2765 and 8.2770 over the previous two weeks, closing six times outside its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  The central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in the band over the past three years, partly to quell speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced its activity over the past few months, allowing the yuan to end outside the wafer-thin range nearly 20 times since Mid-April. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the bank was testing the waters for a gradual widening of the band as China draws close to membership in the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The government has pledged to pursue gradually a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to prepare for China's accession to the global trade body, but has given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan strengthened against the Japanese yen to close at 7.3775, up from 7.6260 on Tuesday. It ended marginally firmer against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0606 compared with 1.0608 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbm012f9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
