\par\par {\*\bkmkstart toc98}{\*\bkmkend toc98}\b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China yuan closes strong, outside trading band. \b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2000\par 05:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 9 (Reuters) - China's yuan strayed outside the firm end of its sliver-thin trading band for the third time in less than a week on Thursday, closing a touch stronger on continued dollar selling by Chinese exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2768 to the dollar, outside the band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long enforced. The yuan ended at 8.2769 on Wednesday and 8.2768 last Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan movements today were dominated by market trades, in particular dollar selling from exporters," said a dealer at a U.S. bank. "There was no \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen." \par \par \uc2  The central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept the yuan within its tight band for more than three years, although it has become less active this year. The yuan has closed outside the band more than a dozen times since mid-April. \par \par \uc2  The yuan opened at 8.2770 on Thursday and moved in a narrow range of 8.2767 and 8.2772 for the entire session. Several dealers said they saw no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But some said there was dollar buying whenever the yuan breached the 8.2768 level and that they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have set this as a new level for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Dollar buying at the 8.2768 level was persistent and helped to cap the yuan's rise. This might be \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar settlement by exporters has pushed the yuan higher over the past few weeks. Chinese firms usually export more goods to Western countries in October and November to meet strong demand during the Christmas and New Year seasons. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible only on the current account. Banks usually settle their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the national market after trading the yuan with their corporate clients, mostly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be taking advantage of the yuan's strength to test a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  The bank takes a harder line when the yuan is soft, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop it from ending weaker than 8.2800 to prevent speculation of a devaluation, they said. \par \par \uc2  Officials say China intends to make its rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime more flexible to cushion against shocks arising from the country's accession to the World Trade Organisation but give no timetable. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters poll of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts in July, six of 10 respondents said China would allow the yuan to trade in a broader range of at least plus or minus five percent by the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to remain strong in the next few sessions, moving mainly between 8.2765 and 8.2770. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan weakened against the yen to close at 7.8010 to 100 yen versus Wednesday's 7.6340. It ended stronger against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0604 from 1.0617. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb902d47\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
